loan_history:
[< Slovak/Czech] < Latin olīva < Greek ἐλαίϝα < some Mediterranean source (cf. Buck 1949: 380);
< Slovak/Czech < Latin oliva < Greek elaia (Holub & Kopečný 1952: 254);
< Slovak/Czech < Latin oliva < Greek elaia < probably pre-European (Holub & Lyer 1978: 318)

comment_on_borrowed:
two scenarios possible: 
1) straight from Arabic under the asumption of rare m > b correspondence;
2) ultimately from Latin sūbula “awl“ through Berber c.f. Tamsheq tasūbala (with Berber femine prefix ta- ) and metathesis s <=> b

